//! A first x86-64 backend for the SSA form (docs/jit/ssa.md) under the compiled-entry ABI
//! (docs/jit/compiled-entry.md, js_compiled.CompiledCode):
//!   extern "C" fn(arguments: *const ValueBits, state: *mut u64, counter: *mut i32, data: ?*mut u8,
//!     env: ?*mut CompiledEnv, frame: *const CompiledFrame) -> u32
//! Integers, bools and doubles live in registers where x64_regalloc finds one, else in their
//! slot; a boxed `value` always lives in its slot. Every instruction reads its operands into
//! scratch registers, computes there, and writes its result last, after any exit. An edge is a
//! parallel move from its arguments' places to the target's parameters'.
//!
//! Values. Every SSA value has a 16-byte slot. An i32 value's slot holds it zero-extended in
//! its first word, a bool 0 or 1. A boxed `value` is a ValueBits in the engine's own layout
//! (value_layout.ValueLayout, probed from the engine): the arguments are the caller's cells,
//! the exit state and the return value are ValueBits the engine takes over as they are.
//! Counts are real: `retain` adds one inline when the value is a heap reference (all
//! HeapCell.retain does), `release` calls the engine's deferred release through the helper
//! table, a `move` exit slot or `return` hands the count over, and a `dup` slot adds one.
//!
//! The six ABI inputs are stored in fixed frame slots by the prologue; a helper call clobbers
//! the caller-saved registers, so after one the code reloads arguments, state, counter and data.
//!
//! Frame mode (lowerOnFrame, docs/jit/optimizer.md) lowers the same form for a baseline frame
//! (baseline.silk) in place of the compiled-entry ABI: the code enters through the shared
//! prologue, with r12 the BaselineEnv and r13 the runtime, keeps its value slots in a spill
//! area below the frame's cells, calls through the baseline call stubs, returns through
//! STUB_LEAVE, and at an exit writes its values into the frame's cells and goes on in the
//! function's baseline code (x64_frame_exit). Nothing is pinned but rbp, r12 and r13.
